  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1019 PM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nHigh pressure will dominate the weather for eastern Utah and Western \nColorado through Tuesday before a general zonal flow aloft dominates \nthe pattern with a stronger pressure gradient to the north and \nweaker gradient across the region and to the south. The pwats will \nremain below 0.70 inches through the coming week limiting any \nshowers or thunderstorms that may develop through the mountains to \nremain light with little accumulation. The GFS indicated an inverted \ntrough moving to the southwest under the high this evening that \nsupported isolated light thunder showers over the San Juans, but the \nmodels show the high over the Four Corners strengthening tomorrow \ninto Monday to suppress the chances for afternoon showers and \nthunderstorms over the southern mountains. Though we can't rule out \nthese showers and thunderstorms, we can expect them to be isolated \nand light. There are still increased chances for scattered showers \nand thunderstorms across the southern Colorado mountains Tuesday \nafternoon with more isolated storms to the north into the central \nmountains as a weak upper-level disturbance transits the region \nnorthwest to southeast. Through the remainder of the week, isolated \nlight showers and thunderstorms are possible mostly along the Divide \nwith more scattered storms to the south over the San Juans, though \nconfidence is low. There are indications of chance coming next \nweekend with a deep trough descending down through the Pacific \nNorthwest towards the region bringing cooler weather and chances for \nmore showers. Stay tuned for updates on this system. Otherwise, \nexpect mild dry weather across the region through the coming week \nwith temperatures running five to ten degrees above normal for the \nfirst week of October.",
